// works — 117ms, 4377468 bytes
const a = await fetch(URL_);
await Bun.write("/tmp/ok.tgz", a);

// hangs forever at ~100% CPU; /tmp/hang.tgz is never created
const b = await fetch(URL_);
await Bun.write("/tmp/hang.tgz", new Response(b.body));
```

The response is unremarkable: `content-length: 4377468`, no `content-encoding`, no `transfer-encoding`.

### What is the expected behavior?

`Bun.write` should write the body and resolve, as it does when the `Response` from `fetch` is passed through directly.

### What do you see instead?

The promise never settles. `sample` shows a live JS/stream stack on the main thread (a busy loop, not an I/O wait). No error, no timeout, no bytes written.

Notably, the same re-wrapped `Response` is fine if you consume it yourself — so the `Response`-over-stream construction is not the problem, `Bun.write`'s handling of it is:

```ts
