**Cause.** `parse_functional_pseudo_element` (`src/css/selectors/parser.rs:1216`) knows four named view transition pseudo-elements: `-group()`, `-image-pair()`, `-old()` and `-new()`. css-view-transitions-2 adds a fifth for nested groups, [`::view-transition-group-children()`](https://drafts.csswg.org/css-view-transitions-2/#::view-transition-group-children). It falls to `PseudoElement::CustomFunction`, which warns and keeps the raw tokens. Bun already parses the `view-transition-group` property from the same feature (`src/css/properties/transition.rs:130`).

In plain CSS the output is correct and only the warning is wrong. In a CSS module the rule is dead, as shown above.

**Reference.** Chromium parses the argument of all five with one block (`css_selector_parser.cc`, the `kPseudoViewTransitionGroup` / `kPseudoViewTransitionGroupChildren` / `-ImagePair` / `-Old` / `-New` case), and accepts `:only-child` after all five. lightningcss does not know the fifth one yet.

